WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.413
- Poof, Saul Goodman is there.

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:04.608
(bell dinging)

00:00:04.608 --> 00:00:07.191
(upbeat music)

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:11.870
My agent called me and said,
they're gonna offer you a role.

00:00:12.834 --> 00:00:15.500
It's a great show and you
should say yes to doing it

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.090
and, I called a friend.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.710
Not many people had seen
Breaking Bad at that point.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:24.880
It was towards the end
of season two shooting.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.710
The first season was cut
short by a writer's strike

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:31.970
and I think the show was pretty
much overshadowed by Mad Men

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:35.270
to a great, great extent,
and it hadn't streamed.

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:38.990
So, very few people
knew Breaking Bad until

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:40.253
about the fourth season.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:45.850
So, I did actually call a
friend I'd been working with

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:48.213
and I said, "Do you know
the show Breaking Bad?,"

00:00:48.213 --> 00:00:50.877
and he said, "Best show on TV.

00:00:50.877 --> 00:00:52.600
"You've gotta say yes to that."

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.290
So I said, "Sure, I'll do it."

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:59.130
You know, it's, I knew it was
lawyer who's kind of a con man

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:03.820
and that just sounded fun and
totally within my wheelhouse

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.210
'cause I played Stevie
Grant on Larry Sanders

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.660
and it just sounded fun and
I said, "Sure, I'll do it,"

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.040
and then they sent me the script

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:13.410
and it had a lot of lines.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.670
You know, it had all these long
runs, you know, of dialogue

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.030
and I figured they'd cut those way back,

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:22.460
because in comedy, you don't
tend to monologue like that

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.110
and then Vince got on the phone with me,

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.287
and because I had read the script, I said,

00:01:27.287 --> 00:01:29.027
"I have an idea for this guy's hair.

00:01:29.027 --> 00:01:32.430
"I think he should have a comb
over and a mullet in back."

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:34.667
And Vince said, "That sounds like fun.

00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:36.280
"I like that idea."

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:37.730
Which was really cool of him.

00:01:38.681 --> 00:01:43.010
And, I mean I was scheduled
to do three or four episodes.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:46.180
They did say they needed me for four,

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:49.453
and I couldn't do the
fourth episode because of,

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:53.720
I was already signed up to
do How I Met Your Mother

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:57.150
and so they invented the
character of Mike Ehrmantraut,

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:01.730
played by Jonathan Banks,
to carry that plot work

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:05.210
that they needed done
in that fourth episode

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:08.140
towards the end of season two.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:10.360
I think the last episode
it would have been.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.320
But hey, they created Mike
'cause of How I Met Your Mother

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.434
so, if you like Mike, thank
How I Met Your Mother.

00:02:17.434 --> 00:02:18.267
(foot stomping)

00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:21.650
Better call Saul! (laughing)

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:26.500
The first scene I did on
Breaking Bad with Bryan Cranston,

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.380
was a big scene where I tell a
monologue about the character

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:32.560
and tell Walter White who
I am and kind of tell him

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.370
he doesn't need me and why
don't you just kill your friend

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:36.380
who's in jail.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.960
Somebody in the crew joked,
"Can I get a job on the sequel?"

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.840
After we shot that scene.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.630
Right there on the set.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.730
Somebody shouted out, "Can
I get a job on the sequel?"

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:48.470
And everyone laughed because I guess

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:49.720
the character really popped.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.160
Everyone saw the character
as a big, big show

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:54.910
and it was a joke.

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:59.170
And then, a few weeks
later, Vince was in the,

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:00.470
I don't know if it was a few weeks later

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:03.240
or I think it was a year later, when Saul

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.667
was coming back and people would joke,

00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:07.277
"Do you think he should have a sequel?

00:03:07.277 --> 00:03:09.430
"I think I'd like to watch that."

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:12.937
Vince said to me, "Do
you think there's a show

00:03:12.937 --> 00:03:14.267
"in this character?

00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:16.940
"'Cause I think there is," he said.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:20.273
And, I said, "I don't know, you know.

00:03:20.273 --> 00:03:22.920
"If you think there is, I guess."

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:23.753
I gotta be honest.

00:03:23.753 --> 00:03:25.900
I did not think so, not at the time.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.780
'Cause he's just this con man, who cares.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:31.460
I think in the world of Breaking Bad,

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:36.460
I was very aware that
Saul was more fun to watch

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.850
because the stakes were low for Saul

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.120
for most of the run of the series.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.850
Nobody's trying to kil him.

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:45.640
Everyone else is gonna die.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.510
Everyone is at some
point losing their family

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.120
or they're gonna die.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:53.750
Not Saul, so it's big,
it's a big game for him,

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:56.360
so he's fun to watch and
he can make wise cracks

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:59.077
and he has a lighter energy
and so people are like,

00:03:59.077 --> 00:04:01.010
"Oh, I like Saul and he's funny."

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:05.310
And yeah, he's funny in relation
to these horrible people

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:09.360
who are in hell, but
alone is he fun to watch?

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.540
I don't think he's really
worth watching alone.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.230
And when they talked,
Vince and Peter talked

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:17.407
about creating a series, I said,

00:04:17.407 --> 00:04:18.930
"You gotta make him likable."

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:20.390
I don't think he's a likable guy.

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:23.880
He's likable in relation
to the world around him

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:24.730
in Breaking Bad.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:29.030
He's fun to watch, but they
went ahead and did that.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:32.480
They invented who he
was, who he really was.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:35.843
Who James McGill was, and
James McGill is likable.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.890
Saul is a front and a
facade and I don't know

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:43.040
if you could say you like
that version of a person,

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:45.680
but the real guy that we've gotten to know

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:48.220
in Better Call Saul is a likable guy

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:51.330
who you can empathize with and champion.

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.870
How do I get 'em to call Jimmy McGill?

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:54.703
I don't!

00:04:54.703 --> 00:04:56.410
I stay Saul Goodman.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:58.110
They call the guy they already know.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.470
Well, it's a vastly different performance

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:02.810
in Better Call Saul than in Breaking Bad.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:06.380
It's way, 100 million times different.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:10.360
The good thing we had going
for us was in Breaking Bad,

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:14.930
he told Walter White and
he told the audience,

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:16.750
this isn't who I really am.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:19.750
But then, he never
showed who he really was.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:21.440
You never saw him go home.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.913
You don't know what his personal life was.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:26.980
And there were a few times in the course

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:29.610
of the Breaking Bad story where he

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.723
showed a deeper dimension.

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:37.030
One time, when he's in the car with Jesse

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:40.380
and a few other of Jesse's friends

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:44.520
and he's dropped off some gifts for Rock

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.120
and then he goes and tells Jesse,

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:47.970
you should go in and talk to them.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:50.730
And he kind of insists that you should.

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:54.000
Now that's not Saul
talking, that's James McGill

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.410
because there is nothing
in there for Saul.

00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:59.480
There's only danger in it for Saul

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:04.100
to encourage him to go
talk to these people.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:07.240
And so, that's a moment where you see

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:10.570
this other character peek
through and then at some point,

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:14.220
he also tells Walter White
to quit while he's ahead

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:17.220
which is also not Saul,
because Saul doesn't care

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.140
if Walter White gets killed.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:22.550
He just wants to make money,
so when he tells Walter White,

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:25.450
you know, some people would say,

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:30.080
your position maybe should just back off

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:32.063
and go back to life.

00:06:33.146 --> 00:06:35.680
And, that's not Saul talking.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:39.280
So there were these moments
in the run of Breaking Bad

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:41.500
where you saw Jimmy McGill.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:42.620
- I'm what?

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:44.453
- You're not a real lawyer!

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.260
- It's between, you know, Chuck and Kim.

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:51.710
Those are two really deep

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:56.320
and very well fleshed out
relationships in Better Call Saul.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:01.320
Chuck is probably the most
interesting and idiosyncratic,

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:04.900
'cause you just don't
see that often played out

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:05.870
in a long form.

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:09.300
It's a sibling relationship
that is that deep and

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.700
that scarred and intense.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:14.650
Those guys are way too close and,

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:16.190
they should leave each other alone,

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:19.700
but they, they both need
something from the other one

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:22.270
and they're not gonna get it.

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:27.270
And Chuck kills himself
as a way to kind of, win.

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:28.814
- [Kim] Jimmy, that's not--

00:07:28.814 --> 00:07:30.600
- Yeah, you look at me and
you see Slipping Jimmy.

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:31.433
- I never said that!

00:07:31.433 --> 00:07:32.630
- Yeah, but you thought it!

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:37.110
You know, Kim and Jimmy, that
is an amazing relationship

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:41.650
that more than anything in the show,

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:46.650
I think, shows some maturation
from the character of Jimmy.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.463
And shows the character growing up

00:07:50.463 --> 00:07:52.090
and I like that a lot.

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:54.170
You know, I'm playing a
character who's much younger

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:57.920
than myself, and one of
the hardest things to play,

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:00.940
I can't do much about
my face and all that,

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:04.660
the makeup people work
on it and do their best,

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:08.640
and the audience has been incredibly kind,

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:13.073
to kind of suspend disbelief
and let me play this guy.

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:16.750
But, one of the hardest
things is to play his level

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:19.960
of immaturity and his outlook on life

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:22.090
and towards other people.

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:27.090
As a person, I read his
part, or I have at times

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.630
and gone, "Will you just fucking grow up?"

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:31.463
You know?

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:36.460
But it's a story of a
guy struggling to grow up

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:40.660
and as the time's gone by
and he has changed and arced,

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:43.370
now we get into places
that I can relate to more

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:44.570
and that I can feel,

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:47.180
personally I can feel more sympathy for

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:48.230
and it's really great.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:50.020
I really like it.

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:51.130
- If this is how you're really feeling.

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:51.963
- [Bob] It is.

00:08:51.963 --> 00:08:52.970
- I say sure.

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:54.970
- [Bob] Great, five minutes max.

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:57.280
And then the relationship with Kim,

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:00.330
you know, this last season, season four,

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:04.490
there's these moments where
Jimmy is honest with Kim

00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:06.150
and he just tells her truth.

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:09.930
He doesn't try to hide it
or con her or just escape

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:12.330
without being mature about it,

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:15.720
and he tells her the
truth and she listens.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:20.380
And those things are, those
are, those are the great signs

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:25.380
of two characters growing and
of a couple that should work.

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:28.260
I mean they should be able to make it work

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:31.070
which is of course gonna
be tragic if they can't

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:32.710
which I assume they can't.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:36.370
I mean, I assume something big goes wrong

00:09:36.370 --> 00:09:38.620
and of course I don't
wanna give anyway spoilers

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:41.410
but I'm seriously talking as a fan,

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:46.410
that I don't think that
Saul Goodman in Breaking Bad

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:50.080
is going home to Kim Wexler.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:52.643
I dread what could happen to her.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:56.300
But if something really tragic happens,

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:58.310
then I don't see how he could go on,

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:00.870
so I don't know, I
don't know what happens.

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:03.550
And I almost think it
would be more tragic,

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:06.810
and I'm just spit balling here as a fan,

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:10.170
if she and he were separated
but still carrying on

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:12.220
in Albuquerque and he, you know,

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:14.320
she had to drive by his billboard.

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:16.203
He had to drive by her law office.

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:24.970
I think it's easier to
maintain now than it was

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.940
and the reason is because
in the character's story

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:31.800
he's decided to very
consciously compartmentalize

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:36.060
his worst instincts and call
them Saul Goodman and be that

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:39.640
and when he's that, he's
that, and when he's home,

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:42.370
he's Jimmy McGill and he's a person

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:45.590
that Kim Wexler can trust and love

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:48.180
and so, because he's now made this choice,

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:51.300
like I'm not gonna just
kind of go through life

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:53.740
and struggle with all these urges,

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:56.640
I'm gonna put all my bad urges right here.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:01.230
I'm gonna dress for it
and I'm gonna be that guy

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:03.800
and don't expect anything good from him

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:06.530
and then when I go home,
I'll take off my tie

00:11:06.530 --> 00:11:11.530
and my lime green socks and I
will be a good, this good guy

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:16.100
who, you know, is capable
of honest interaction

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:21.070
and caring and so in a way,
it's gotten easier now.

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:23.790
Because it's easier for Jimmy.

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:26.040
I mean I think the character feels lighter

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:29.617
and more energetic because
he goes, "I figured it out.

00:11:29.617 --> 00:11:30.937
"I figured it out.

00:11:30.937 --> 00:11:32.347
"I know what I have to do.

00:11:32.347 --> 00:11:33.970
"I have to play this part."

00:11:33.970 --> 00:11:38.970
So I, I find season five
to be a more upbeat,

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:45.610
energetic, and kind of, he's
clearer in what he's doing

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:48.210
and I think he's grown emotionally.

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:50.830
Now, the question is, can
a person make that work

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:53.570
in the real world or can a person do that?

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:54.940
That's what they're asking.

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:58.040
You know, can you separate
yourself out like that

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:01.695
and live a bifurcated existence like that?

00:12:01.695 --> 00:12:03.695
And we'll, we'll see what the show says.

00:12:04.611 --> 00:12:07.194
(gentle music)

